(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pharm-e3-drill-antiarrhythmics"]={"title":"Antiarrhythmic Drugs Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class shortens phase 3 and decreases the automaticity of ectopic foci?","choices":["Class IB","Class IA","Class IC","Class III"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IB (lidocaine) shortens phase 3, giving a shorter action potential, and decreases ectopic foci automaticity, acting mainly in ischemic tissue. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class affects the normal heart, slowing phase 0 and conduction?","choices":["Class IB","Class IC","Class II","Class IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Class IC slows phase 0, slows conduction, prolongs the refractory period and affects the normal heart, unlike class IB. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class decreases phase 4 depolarization by blocking beta receptors?","choices":["Class IB","Class IA","Class II","Class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class II drugs are beta receptor antagonists: they slow conduction, decrease automaticity, prolong atrioventricular conduction and decrease phase 4 depolarization. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class prolongs phase 3 repolarization by reducing potassium efflux?","choices":["Class II","Class IB","Class IV","Class III"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Class III drugs reduce potassium efflux, prolonging phase 3 repolarization, the refractory period and the duration of the action potential. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class blocks L-type calcium channels and slows atrioventricular node conduction?","choices":["Class IV","Class IA","Class IC","Class IB"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IV calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) block the L-type channel, decrease automaticity, slow atrioventricular node conduction and are negative inotropes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes cinchonism: tinnitus, dizziness and blurred vision?","choices":["Procainamide","Quinidine","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cinchonism (tinnitus, dizziness, blurred vision) is on quinidine's toxicity list; quinidine is the D-isomer of quinine. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is the D-isomer of quinine?","choices":["Procainamide","Disopyramide","Quinidine","Flecainide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine is the D-isomer of quinine (brands Quinalan, Cardioquin, Quinidex), which is why it can cause cinchonism. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic inhibits cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6), affecting codeine and morphine?","choices":["Procainamide","Disopyramide","Lidocaine","Quinidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine's listed clinical interaction is inhibition of cytochrome P450 2D6 (CYP2D6), with codeine and morphine as the named examples. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes nausea, vomiting and diarrhea in 30 to 50 percent of patients?","choices":["Quinidine","Lidocaine","Flecainide","Disopyramide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea occur in 30 to 50 percent of patients on quinidine, its most frequent toxicity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic acts as an alpha blocker at higher doses?","choices":["Procainamide","Quinidine","Flecainide","Lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine's indirect actions are anticholinergic effects and alpha blockade at higher doses, which contributes to hypotension. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause thrombocytopenia that may be fatal?","choices":["Lidocaine","Disopyramide","Quinidine","Dofetilide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine's toxicity list includes thrombocytopenia, marked as potentially fatal, alongside immunological reactions. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes a lupus-like syndrome, more often in slow acetylators?","choices":["Quinidine","Disopyramide","Flecainide","Procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A lupus-like syndrome occurs in 25 to 50 percent of patients on procainamide, more often in slow acetylators. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic forms an active acetylated metabolite with class III activity?","choices":["Procainamide","Quinidine","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide has an active acetylated metabolite (N-acetylprocainamide, NAPA) with class III activity; fast acetylators may increase it. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has a short 3 to 4 hour duration of action, so a slow-release preparation is used?","choices":["Flecainide","Procainamide","Amiodarone","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide has a short duration of action of 3 to 4 hours, so it comes as a slow-release preparation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is an amide derivative of procaine?","choices":["Quinidine","Disopyramide","Procainamide","Flecainide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide (Procan, Pronestyl) is an amide derivative of procaine, with less anticholinergic activity than quinidine. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause bone marrow suppression in 0.2 percent of patients?","choices":["Quinidine","Lidocaine","Amiodarone","Procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide's adverse effects include bone marrow suppression (0.2 percent), with the lupus-like syndrome and torsades de pointes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has effects similar to quinidine but more anticholinergic action?","choices":["Disopyramide","Procainamide","Lidocaine","Propafenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide's effects are similar to quinidine, with more anticholinergic actions and a negative inotropic effect. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is contraindicated in glaucoma?","choices":["Procainamide","Disopyramide","Lidocaine","Amiodarone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide is contraindicated in heart block, hypersensitivity and glaucoma; its strong anticholinergic action explains the glaucoma. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause congestive heart failure by decreasing contractility?","choices":["Quinidine","Procainamide","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide has negative inotropic effects, and its toxicity list includes congestive heart failure from decreased contractility. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has nystagmus as an early sign of toxicity?","choices":["Quinidine","Flecainide","Amiodarone","Lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine's central nervous system toxicity includes drowsiness, confusion, restlessness, muscle twitches and seizures, with nystagmus as an early sign. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is given intravenously because of extensive first-pass metabolism?","choices":["Lidocaine","Flecainide","Amiodarone","Procainamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is given intravenously because of extensive first-pass metabolism, so it is not taken by mouth. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic works best on sodium channels in ischemic tissue, with less effect on normal tissue?","choices":["Flecainide","Lidocaine","Quinidine","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is most effective on sodium channels in ischemic tissue and has less effect on normal tissue; acute myocardial infarction is among its indications. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which sodium channel blocker is not effective against atrial arrhythmias?","choices":["Quinidine","Flecainide","Lidocaine","Disopyramide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is not effective against atrial arrhythmias; its indications are ventricular tachycardia, premature beats, ventricular fibrillation and acute myocardial infarction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has seizures and age as contraindications?","choices":["Quinidine","Disopyramide","Amiodarone","Lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine's listed contraindications are seizures and age; its central nervous system toxicity itself can progress to seizures. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic increases mortality in myocardial infarction?","choices":["Flecainide","Lidocaine","Propranolol","Amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list includes increased mortality in myocardial infarction, and it is used only without significant structural heart damage. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic should be used only in patients without significant structural damage to the heart?","choices":["Lidocaine","Flecainide","Amiodarone","Dofetilide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide is used only in patients without significant structural damage to the heart, because it increases mortality in myocardial infarction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic prolongs the PR, QRS and QT intervals?","choices":["Lidocaine","Magnesium sulfate","Flecainide","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles and prolongs the PR, QRS and QT intervals. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause tremor?","choices":["Disopyramide","Metoprolol","Dofetilide","Flecainide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide's toxicity list begins with blurred vision and tremor, then resistant ventricular tachycardia, bronchospasm, seizures and thrombocytopenia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles and slows recovery of the sodium channel?","choices":["Flecainide","Lidocaine","Dofetilide","Diltiazem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flecainide blocks sodium and potassium channels in the ventricles and slows recovery of the sodium channel, prolonging the PR, QRS and QT intervals. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has the same mechanism as flecainide plus beta-blocking activity?","choices":["Sotalol","Propafenone","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone has the same mechanism as flecainide plus beta-blocking activity, which brings bradycardia, bronchospasm and worse heart failure. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause agranulocytosis?","choices":["Sotalol","Lidocaine","Propafenone","Amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Propafenone's toxicity list includes agranulocytosis, anemia and thrombocytopenia, along with proarrhythmia and bronchospasm. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is nonselective and membrane stabilizing?","choices":["Metoprolol","Acebutolol","Esmolol","Propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol is a nonselective beta blocker that reduces phase 4 activation, slows atrioventricular conduction and is membrane stabilizing. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is given intravenously and is short acting?","choices":["Esmolol","Propranolol","Acebutolol","Sotalol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Esmolol is the intravenous, short-acting beta blocker listed beside the beta-1 selective agents metoprolol and acebutolol. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is beta-1 selective, with less bronchospasm than propranolol?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Sotalol","Propafenone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metoprolol is a beta-1 selective blocker similar to propranolol but with less bronchospasm; it can still cause heart failure. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is an analog of thyroid hormone?","choices":["Dofetilide","Sotalol","Amiodarone","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is an analog of thyroid hormone, given orally and intravenously, which explains its thyroid abnormalities (hyper or hypo). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has a half-life of about 40 days, so loading doses may be needed?","choices":["Flecainide","Procainamide","Adenosine","Amiodarone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's half-life is about 40 days (26 to 107), with slow onset and slow elimination, so it may require loading doses. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes a blue-gray skin color and photosensitivity?","choices":["Amiodarone","Quinidine","Procainamide","Dofetilide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's toxicity list includes blue-gray skin color with photosensitivity, plus corneal deposits and thyroid abnormalities. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes corneal deposits?","choices":["Quinidine","Amiodarone","Disopyramide","Lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corneal deposits are on amiodarone's toxicity list, with blue-gray skin, photosensitivity and thyroid abnormalities. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause potentially fatal pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":["Sotalol","Propranolol","Amiodarone","Dofetilide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's toxicity list includes pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis, marked as potentially fatal, alongside hepatic dysfunction. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic increases digoxin, warfarin, flecainide, phenytoin and procainamide?","choices":["Dofetilide","Sotalol","Lidocaine","Amiodarone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone's listed interactions are increases in digoxin, warfarin, flecainide, phenytoin and procainamide. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic blocks potassium, sodium and calcium channels plus alpha and beta receptors?","choices":["Amiodarone","Sotalol","Dofetilide","Propafenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone blocks potassium, sodium and calcium channels and alpha and beta receptors, decreasing automaticity and conduction and prolonging refractory periods. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug, given intravenously, terminates acute ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation?","choices":["Flecainide","Amiodarone","Verapamil","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous amiodarone terminates acute ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation; it is also listed for recurrent ventricular arrhythmias resistant to other drugs. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is a potent, pure potassium channel blocker?","choices":["Amiodarone","Sotalol","Dofetilide","Flecainide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide (Tikosyn) is a potent pure potassium channel blocker, used for normal rhythm in atrial fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has limited availability, restricted to those with special training?","choices":["Amiodarone","Sotalol","Flecainide","Dofetilide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide has limited availability to those with special training; its adverse effects are torsades de pointes and marked QT prolongation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is mainly eliminated unchanged by the kidneys?","choices":["Dofetilide","Amiodarone","Lidocaine","Verapamil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide is mainly eliminated unchanged in the kidneys, so kidney function matters when it is used. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes marked QT prolongation, with torsades de pointes in 1 to 3 percent?","choices":["Lidocaine","Dofetilide","Esmolol","Diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dofetilide's adverse effects are torsades de pointes (1 to 3 percent) and marked QT prolongation, from pure potassium channel block. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which beta blocker also inhibits potassium channels?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Sotalol","Esmolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol (Betapace) has beta-blocking activity and inhibits potassium channels, so it carries both class II and class III action. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes both torsades de pointes and bronchospasm?","choices":["Dofetilide","Propranolol","Procainamide","Sotalol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sotalol's toxicity is torsades de pointes from potassium channel block plus bronchospasm from its beta-blocking activity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic undergoes hepatic metabolism, so it calls for caution in liver damage?","choices":["Verapamil","Dofetilide","Adenosine","Magnesium sulfate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil undergoes hepatic metabolism, so caution is needed in liver damage; hypotension and edema are its adverse effects. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic should be avoided in ventricular arrhythmias, sick sinus syndrome and depressed cardiac function?","choices":["Amiodarone","Diltiazem","Lidocaine","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem, like verapamil, is to be avoided in ventricular arrhythmias, sick sinus syndrome and depressed cardiac function. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is a calcium channel blocker that suppresses the sinoatrial node?","choices":["Propranolol","Lidocaine","Verapamil","Dofetilide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that is most effective in the most active tissue, decreases atrioventricular conduction and suppresses the sinoatrial node. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds?","choices":["Digoxin","Magnesium sulfate","Esmolol","Adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine hyperpolarizes cells and decreases conduction velocity, and it may stop the heart for 2 to 3 seconds. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic has a half-life of about 10 seconds?","choices":["Adenosine","Lidocaine","Flecainide","Digoxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine's half-life is about 10 seconds, so its effects (and its flushing and chest burning) pass quickly. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic causes flushing in about 20 percent, with shortness of breath and burning in the chest?","choices":["Digoxin","Adenosine","Lidocaine","Procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine's listed toxicity is flushing (20 percent), shortness of breath and burning in the chest. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic activates potassium channels in the atria and hyperpolarizes cells?","choices":["Dofetilide","Amiodarone","Adenosine","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine activates potassium channels in the atria and blocks calcium channels, hyperpolarizing cells and decreasing conduction velocity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase and indirectly increases parasympathetic tone?","choices":["Adenosine","Verapamil","Propranolol","Digoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin (digitalis) inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase, slows atrioventricular conduction and indirectly increases parasympathetic tone while decreasing sympathetic tone. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is used to induce heart block in atrial flutter or fibrillation?","choices":["Digoxin","Dofetilide","Quinidine","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digoxin's indications are to slow the ventricular rate and to induce heart block in atrial flutter and fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic should be avoided with calcium channel blockers and beta blockers?","choices":["Lidocaine","Digoxin","Magnesium sulfate","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers and beta blockers are to be avoided with digoxin, which already slows atrioventricular conduction and can induce heart block. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which drug is the listed treatment for torsades de pointes?","choices":["Amiodarone","Adenosine","Magnesium sulfate","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Magnesium sulfate, given intravenously, is the listed indication for torsades de pointes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic can cause respiratory paralysis?","choices":["Amiodarone","Sotalol","Adenosine","Magnesium sulfate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Magnesium sulfate's toxicities are bradycardia, flushing, headache and respiratory paralysis. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
